A||||21||Indiana_resto_guy||INFO ON BOSTON NEEDED !||||1||
Z||000000||Indiana_resto_guy||08-03-2001||02:47 PM||||HI All!<P>Am planning a trip to Boston and need info on some Hotel/Motel rates.<BR>The only info I have so far is $200.00 a night!?<BR>There must be something cheaper, looking for an overnight stay on the 11th of Aug. (Sat. night Sun. morning) 4 two.<BR>Need names and numbers with any info you may have, closer to the AmTrak station with close food would be best.<P>Thanks for any help!<BR>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jaygee79||08-03-2001||03:05 PM||||You're much better off staying just outside of Boston.  Try  <A HREF="http://boston.hotelguide.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://boston.hotelguide.net/</A>  You can find hotels in the area and check their rates.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR>ICQ: 85931334</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||08-03-2001||03:07 PM||||Cool JC! I'll check that out, Thanks!<P>Anyone else?||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||08-03-2001||04:27 PM||||You don't really need to get a hotel near the train station (South Station), as long as it's somewhere close to a stop on the MBTA. If you get too far away from a subway stop you'll spend a fortune on cabs.  The Red Line also stops at South Station.<P>I took a quick look at travelocity.com for 1 room for 2 the night of 8/11, and came up with a couple of rooms under $200:<P>Wyndham Tremont, 275 Tremont St, $139/night<BR>Club Quarters Boston, 161 Devonshire St, $119.95<P>Both of these would be within reasonable walking distance to the Orange Line (subway), the theatre district, or Quincy Market.<P>I saw several hotels on Memorial Drive in Cambridge as well, but they're all a 1-2 mile walk from their nearest stop (Kendall or Central Square).<P>BTW, you can find a map of Boston's subway system at <A HREF="http://www.mbta.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mbta.com</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||08-03-2001||04:32 PM||||WOAH!!!<P>You know, I didn't even think about the transit system. What an idea!<P>Is there a link for city streets so that I can look for location? I hope so!<P>Thanks LarryB!<P>? . . . . . . .more||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jaygee79||08-03-2001||04:43 PM||||There are also some lines outside of Boston, so you may be able to find something outside of Boston near a line.  I personally thing you would be better staying outside of Boston because the city is nuts and the prices are way higher than they should be.  if you go outside of Boston then its much more reasonable.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR>ICQ: 85931334</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88CoupeV6||08-03-2001||04:51 PM||||Stick to the transit system too. It is nice and cheap. The taxi's rates are outrageous. They will break you fast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> There are lots of things to do, I suggest <A HREF="http://www.ducktours.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ducktours.com/</A>  and the Sam Adams brewery. The view from the Prudential and John Hancock buildings are awesome too. We also have lots of great museums like the museum of science<P>Here is a cool link also <A HREF="http://travel.boston.com/boston/boston_tourist/" TARGET=_blank>http://travel.boston.com/boston/boston_tourist/</A>  It tells you the T(Subway) stops for different attractions<BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000007||LarryB||08-03-2001||05:12 PM||||Are you looking for something like mapquest or maps.yahoo.com to find where in the city a street or address is, or are you just looking for a street map of Boston online?<P>BTW, the problem with staying outside the city is that you're dependent on the commuter rail (short range diesel trains).  Not a bad thing during the week, but during the weekends they only run once every 3 hours, so if you goof up or are in a rush and need to take a cab you'll get raped. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jaygee79||08-03-2001||05:22 PM||||True, but if you stay in Boston, even the Howard Johnson is about $180 a night.  Either way it's a bit of $$, you just have to decide which you would rather deal with.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||08-04-2001||09:58 AM||||Thanks all! I'm going to check out all the possibilities for lodging. My uncle (84 yrs) and I are taking the annual train trip the end of next week. We are planning on taking a Gray Line tour possibly to Cape Cod or something that takes in the USS Constitution in the harbor. I don't think that there would be much to see of interest at Plymouth rock any more.||||reg||1
Z||000010||LarryB||08-04-2001||12:17 PM||||If you want to catch some sightseeing 88coupeV6 hit the nail on the head with DuckTours; they use reconditioned WWII-vintage amphibious vehicles to tour around Boston including a trip in the Charles River.  I've never done it but a few of my friends have and they all thought it was great.<P>There's another tour that takes you around the city in what looks like a trolley; it leaves right in front of the Kendall Square T stop.  From what I've been told you can get off the trolley if you want at one of their stops and pick up a later one to continue the tour.<P>The USS Constitution is about a 10-15 minute walk from North Station up Causeway St and across the N. Washington St. bridge over into Charlestown, or you can take the 10 minute boat shuttle from Long Wharf over to the Charlestown Navy Yard.  It leaves every half hour from 10AM-6PM on weekends.<P>Heading to the Cape on weekends from Boston is tough in the summer. Way too much traffic for the roads to handle.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||08-05-2001||12:56 PM||||All great info, thanks to all!<BR>Anyone know the relation of the AmTrak station location in perspective to the nearest station (by name) of local trains or subway lines?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Boomtastic||08-05-2001||02:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>WOAH!!!<P>You know, I didn't even think about the transit system. What an idea!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Green Line is the only one I would ride on. The Blue Line isn't that bad either, but you take your chances with the rest .. (Red, Orange, etc.)<P>Search for neighboring town as far away as Braintree, and you'll save money. 15 miles away is only 10~15 minutes on the expressway or Mass. Pike. I live 1½ hours away from the city and commute every day into town, if that will give you any idea of how far away to start looking.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> j/k<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||13
Z||000013||Boomtastic||08-05-2001||02:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Heading to the Cape on weekends from Boston is tough in the summer. Way too much traffic for the roads to handle.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LarryB - It's not that bad anymore since Rt 495 has been "extended" down to the Bourne Bridge. Rt. 3 is (will always be) a nightmare getting onto the Cape, and Rt 6. OFF the Cape (on a Sunday) is a joke. Thankfully we locals know the back roads, so curse as much as you want - it's all tourists around you when you're stuck in traffic! Hee hee hee!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000014||Boomtastic||08-05-2001||02:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>All great info, thanks to all!<BR>Anyone know the relation of the AmTrak station location in perspective to the nearest station (by name) of local trains or subway lines?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The AmTrak line runs right into South Station (on the corner of Atlantic Ave & Summer Street), where you can catch a transit bus or the red line subway (across the street).<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick's Dad||08-05-2001||08:13 PM||||IRG: Also check out the brand new Marriott in Crown Colony Park in Quincy (KWIN-zee, not KWINCE-ee ), about ten miles south of Boston. My wife says $169/night. The Red Line (Quincy Adams) Station is about a five minute walk, and they probably have a shuttlebus.<P>Speaking of (John) Quincy Adams, you can see his birthplace, that of his father, his church, and his mansion, among other things in the City of Quincy. If you are really ambitious, you can find the birthplaces of two other presidents not far out of city limits. We do boast the greatest concentration of presidential birthplaces....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jaygee79||08-05-2001||10:47 PM||||Oh, yes.  Make sure you pronounce it Kwinzee and not Kwincee.  Mass people are kinda touchy about that kind of stuff.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR>ICQ: 85931334</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||LarryB||08-05-2001||10:51 PM||||I don't have to curse, I know the path for 24-&gt;495-&gt;25-&gt;28-&gt; Rte 151 and back up. One of my friends grew up in Truro, another in Chatham.  You're right that the Bourne is nowhere as bad as that goldurned rotary before the Sagamore Bridge, but it's all relative - traveling there from Boston on summer weekends rots regardless.   I'm just thinking that a first-timer to Boston coming just for the weekend would see a lot more overall by sticking around metro Boston.<P>Green Line? Bleech. It's the Subway From Hell.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Boomtastic:<BR><B> LarryB - It's not that bad anymore since Rt 495 has been "extended" down to the Bourne Bridge. Rt. 3 is (will always be) a nightmare getting onto the Cape, and Rt 6. OFF the Cape (on a Sunday) is a joke. Thankfully we locals know the back roads, so curse as much as you want - it's all tourists around you when you're stuck in traffic! Hee hee hee!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||lou_dias||08-06-2001||07:59 AM||||Wow, look at all the Massachusetts feiro owning residents on this board...<P>I didn't think we had that much representation here.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Indiana_resto_guy||08-06-2001||04:22 PM||||Thanks for the info!<BR>Reservations at the Logan Airport Hampton Inn for $124.00!<P>Thanks again to those who replied. The subway will come in handy!<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000020||Indiana_resto_guy||08-15-2001||11:05 AM||||Special thanks to LarryB!<P>The subway did the trick! No problems at all and the transportaion was very good. Didn't even need to use any cabs!<BR>My uncle who is 84 said the subway was so fast, you didn't even have time to fart!<P>Took a Gray Line bus tour to Nantuckett with a boat tour (a total of almost 10 hours) It was great! The rain even cooperated with the tour!<BR>We took the back roads to get back to Boston as the Interstate approaches were backed up for miles!  <P>Trip turned out great!<P>Great thanks to ALL who replied to this thread!<P>You have a beautiful city in most places and will be even better when the "Dig" gets completed!<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000021||LarryB||08-15-2001||12:34 PM||||No problem at all, I'm glad it worked out so well for both of you and I'm sure everyone else feels the same way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>This city has its share of problems like everywhere else but it's still a great place to live. It's great you enjoyed yourself so mch. <P>||||reg||1
